.lua
book.chapter(6, "Functions, closures, and application")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Functions are ordinary values"
.pp
A named function lists parameters before :=. An anonymous function uses fn,
parameters, an arrow, and a body. The parser also recognizes fun, but this book
uses fn consistently. Either form constructs a callable value with a type.
It can be stored in a binding, placed in a collection, returned from another
function, or passed to a library operation. The important distinction is
between constructing a function and applying it to arguments.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[add x y := x + y
apply_twice f x := f (f x)
double := fn x -> x * 2
Test.equal "named call" (add 20 22) 42
Test.equal "higher order" (apply_twice double 3) 12
Test.equal "inline lambda" (List.map (fn x -> x + 1) [1, 2]) [2, 3]]=],
    "Named and anonymous function values")
.endlua
.pp
The parentheses around the lambda passed to List.map delimit its body from the
surrounding application. Without that boundary, a reader and parser can have
different ideas about which expression belongs to the lambda. For teaching
and public examples, prefer explicit grouping over the shortest spelling that
happens to parse. The same principle applies when passing a conditional or
match expression as a function argument.
.sh 2 "Currying makes configuration reusable"
.pp
Whitespace application is the idiomatic form: f x y. Supplying fewer arguments
returns a partial application that remembers the supplied values. A type
written A -> B -> C associates to the right, so it can be read as a function
from A to a function from B to C. This viewpoint explains why data-last
libraries fit pipelines: configure the operation first, then provide the data.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[offset amount value := amount + value
add_ten := offset 10
map_ten := List.map add_ten
Test.equal "partial application" (add_ten 5) 15
Test.equal "configured traversal" (map_ten [1, 2, 3]) [11, 12, 13]
Test.equal "pipeline use" ([4, 5] |> map_ten) [14, 15]]=], "Configuration by partial application")
.endlua
.pp
Parenthesized call syntax is supported, but f(x, y) is a multiargument call;
it should not be assumed to pass one tuple. When an API takes a pair, use
whitespace with an explicit tuple or a clearly nested grouping. Currying and
tuple-taking functions have different types even when both eventually consume
two values. Core.curry and Core.uncurry deliberately convert between those
interfaces, as the tuples chapter shows.
.sh 2 "A closure captures what its body needs"
.pp
A closure consists conceptually of code plus an environment. When a function
returns another function, the returned value retains the bindings needed to
evaluate its body later. This is why make_multiplier can finish its own call
without making the chosen factor disappear. The host implementations use
different memory-management strategies, but the language-level result must
remain the same.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[make_multiplier factor := fn value -> factor * value
triple := make_multiplier 3
quadruple := make_multiplier 4
Test.equal "first environment" (triple 7) 21
Test.equal "second environment" (quadruple 7) 28
Test.equal "independent closures" (triple 2, quadruple 2) (6, 8)]=],
    "Two closures from one definition")
book.uml({
    {id="C", name="Closure", members={"parameter patterns", "body expression"}},
    {id="E", name="Environment", members={"captured bindings"}}
}, {{"C", "E", "captures"}}, "A closure's conceptual structure")
.endlua
.pp
Capturing does not imply deep copying every reachable resource. An immutable
list can share a tail, and a foreign table handle can still refer to mutable
storage. A closure using that handle can observe later mutations of the
resource. Likewise, retaining a MetaML function in native code requires the
owning Session to remain alive. Language-level lexical scope and native
resource lifetime are related concerns, but one does not replace the other.
.sh 2 "Patterns as parameters"
.pp
A function parameter can be a pattern rather than a simple variable. A tuple
pattern is convenient for unpacking a fixed product; a record pattern names
fields directly. The pattern is checked against the actual value when the
function is applied. If it fails, evaluation reports a diagnostic. Defining
another function with the same name is not a way to add a fallback clause.
Write one function containing a match when several shapes are acceptable.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[sum_pair (left, right) := left + right
head_or_zero xs := match xs with
  | [] -> 0
  | head :: _ -> head
Test.equal "tuple parameter" (sum_pair (20, 22)) 42
Test.equal "explicit fallback" (head_or_zero []) 0]=], "Destructuring and alternatives")
.endlua
.pp
A partial application delays the body, including its eventual pattern match,
until enough arguments are available. It still evaluates the argument
expressions supplied now. That separation explains why a partially applied
printer can produce effects while being configured if a configuration
expression itself prints, even though the printer's body has not yet run.
Keep configuration expressions pure when you want reusable functions whose
construction has no surprising observable behavior.
.sh 2 "Application versus composition"
.pp
MetaML gives juxtaposition a type-directed interpretation. Ordinary compatible
application is preferred, while supported function-typed situations can resolve
as composition. The checker records that choice for the evaluator. This is a
distinctive feature, but it makes types particularly important when reading a
dense expression of function names. A value of type Int -> Int can transform
an integer; a value of type (Int -> Int) -> Int consumes a function instead.
Similar surface syntax does not make those operations equivalent.
.pp
Use explicit >> or << when building a pipeline function, and |> when passing a
known value through steps. A named intermediate function can make an inferred
interface visible at the prompt with #type. These choices also improve error
locality: a mismatch between two named stages is easier to interpret than a
failure inside a long unparenthesized sequence. Composition is explored in
detail after the chapter on tacit parameter scope.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read the evaluator's application and closure cases, the function type
representation, and the parser's lambda handling in MetaML.hpp. The portable
List.map and Option.map definitions are useful higher-order clients. Implement
make_affine scale offset as a closure, then construct two independently
configured transformations and test them on the same inputs. Finally, change
one interface to accept a tuple and explain why its type is different from
the curried version even though the arithmetic formula is unchanged.
